{
  "notes": [
    "Sample sql_targets.json - where and when each SQL task runs. Copy to sql_targets.json and edit. One row is one script against one instance on one schedule; the same 'sql_id' may appear many times with different targets.",
    "'sql_id' + 'target_no' together identify a row. 'server_id' points at db_instances.json and 'credential_name' at users.json - a target names accounts, never passwords.",
    "'database_name' null means the script decides for itself (its own USE or ALTER SESSION). Set it only when the SQL must run inside a named database and does not say so itself. On SQL Server this is a real database name, unlike the 'service_name' on the instance, which is a label.",
    "'time_window' is the shared scheduling object. 'repeat_interval' -1 means MANUAL ONLY: the task is never scheduled and runs when somebody asks for it. That is the right setting for anything that writes.",
    "'output' decides how the result comes back - 'plain' text or 'xlsx' - and which chat receives it. It is a property of the target rather than of the script, because the same query is a spreadsheet for one audience and a text answer for another.",
    "'notify' is the shared notification object: 'logging_on_run' announces that it ran, 'alert_on_error' announces that it failed, and each names a chat by its routing level. Setting 'chat_id' directly overrides the level lookup and is for the rare case that needs one specific chat.",
    "Confine a heavy task with from_hour/to_hour. A report that scans a large table at 09:00 competes with the people the database exists for."
  ],
